{
    "title": "Consolidation of the Office of the Executive Secretariat Into the Justice Management Division",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 29, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-29/pdf/2025-23880.pdf",
    "summary": "Three end-of-year federal updates aim to buy time, streamline operations, and clean up technical rules. Housing and Urban Development is delaying enforcement of new rules for a home loan guarantee program that serves Native communities, the Justice Department is folding a paperwork and clearance office back into its main management arm, and Homeland Security is making clerical fixes to its published civil penalty amounts for 2025.\n\nFor the home loan program, Housing and Urban Development says it needs more time to finish a comprehensive handbook, consult with Tribes and industry, and train everyone on the changes. A new compliance date will be announced after the handbook is done, with at least 30 to 90 days\u2019 notice before enforcement. This delay gives Tribes, lenders, loan servicers, and other participants more breathing room to update policies, systems, and staff training. Borrowers and communities hoping for quicker clarity and stronger servicing standards may face continued uncertainty, and organizations that already invested to meet the new requirements may be stuck waiting.\n\nInside the Justice Department, the Office of the Executive Secretariat will no longer stand alone. Its work\u2014coordinating and tracking documents that need high-level review and signatures\u2014returns to the Department\u2019s management division, which handled these tasks in the past. Supporters will see this as a common-sense consolidation that cuts overlap and speeds internal approvals, with no direct effect on the public. Others may worry that dissolving a dedicated office could reduce visibility or create short-term confusion during the transition.\n\nHomeland Security\u2019s action is largely housekeeping. It corrects the way 2025 civil penalty amounts are shown in the regulations, following earlier inflation adjustments that are already in effect. The benefit is clarity: fewer mismatches between published rules and the amounts agencies apply. There\u2019s little downside specific to this update, though people and businesses subject to fines should remember that inflation-adjusted penalties remain in place; this step doesn\u2019t lower or raise them, and it doesn\u2019t change policy.\n\nTaken together, these moves are meant to reduce confusion, tighten internal processes, and keep the rulebooks accurate. The trade-off is that some stakeholders get more time and clearer guidance, while others must wait longer for new protections or navigate organizational changes behind the scenes.",
